### What is ClassNotFoundException?

ClassNotFoundException is thrown when you try to load a class dynamically by name and the class is not on the classpath. The class name is a string, so the compiler cannot verify it exists.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ClassNotFoundDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Dynamic loading: class name is a string
        try {
            Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
            System.out.println("Driver loaded");
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("ClassNotFoundException: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Fix: add mysql-connector-j to pom.xml");
        }

        // ClassLoader.loadClass() also throws CNFE
        try {
            ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader().loadClass("com.bank.plugin.CustomParser");
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Plugin not found: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
ClassNotFoundException: com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver
Fix: add mysql-connector-j to pom.xml
Plugin not found: com.bank.plugin.CustomParser
```

### What is NoClassDefFoundError?

NoClassDefFoundError is thrown when the JVM needs a class that was available at compile time but is missing at runtime. This is typically a deployment problem.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class NoClassDefFoundDemo {

    // Imagine this class was compiled with vendor.jar on the classpath:
    // import com.vendor.PaymentGateway;
    // public void pay() {
    //     PaymentGateway gw = new PaymentGateway();  // direct reference
    //     gw.process();
    // }
    // At compile time: PaymentGateway.class existed, code compiled.
    // At runtime: vendor.jar not deployed.
    // Result: NoClassDefFoundError when JVM tries to load PaymentGateway

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("NoClassDefFoundError scenarios:");
        System.out.println("1. JAR present at compile time, missing at runtime");
        System.out.println("2. Static initializer failed on first load");
        System.out.println("3. Class file corrupted or deleted after compilation");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("It is an Error, not an Exception.");
        System.out.println("It means deployment is broken.");
    }
}
```

```text
NoClassDefFoundError scenarios:
1. JAR present at compile time, missing at runtime
2. Static initializer failed on first load
3. Class file corrupted or deleted after compilation

It is an Error, not an Exception.
It means deployment is broken.
```

### How are they different?

| Feature | ClassNotFoundException | NoClassDefFoundError |
|---|---|---|
| Type | Checked Exception | Error (unchecked) |
| When | Dynamic loading (`Class.forName`) | Direct reference at runtime |
| Cause | Class never on classpath | Was on classpath at compile, missing at runtime |
| Compile time | No guarantee (string-based) | Guaranteed (direct import) |
| Fix | Add JAR, fix class name | Fix deployment, include dependency |
| Should catch? | Yes (checked, forced) | No (Error, deployment problem) |
| Category | Expected possibility | Unexpected failure |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ComparisonDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // CNFE: checked Exception
        System.out.println("CNFE superclass: " +
            ClassNotFoundException.class.getSuperclass().getSimpleName());

        // NCDFE: Error
        System.out.println("NCDFE superclass: " +
            NoClassDefFoundError.class.getSuperclass().getSimpleName());

        // CNFE is-a Exception: true
        System.out.println("CNFE is Exception: " +
            Exception.class.isAssignableFrom(ClassNotFoundException.class));

        // NCDFE is-a Error: true
        System.out.println("NCDFE is Error: " +
            Error.class.isAssignableFrom(NoClassDefFoundError.class));
    }
}
```

```text
CNFE superclass: ReflectiveOperationException
NCDFE superclass: LinkageError
CNFE is Exception: true
NCDFE is Error: true
```

### What causes NoClassDefFoundError from a failed static initializer?

If a class's static initializer throws, the JVM marks the class as permanently failed. The first attempt throws ExceptionInInitializerError, and every subsequent attempt throws NoClassDefFoundError.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class StaticInitFailureDemo {

    static class BrokenConfig {
        static final int VALUE = Integer.parseInt("NOT_A_NUMBER");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // First attempt: ExceptionInInitializerError
        try {
            System.out.println(BrokenConfig.VALUE);
        } catch (ExceptionInInitializerError e) {
            System.out.println("First: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
            System.out.println("Cause: " + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // Second attempt: NoClassDefFoundError (class is permanently dead)
        try {
            System.out.println(BrokenConfig.VALUE);
        } catch (NoClassDefFoundError e) {
            System.out.println("Second: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
            System.out.println("Original cause is GONE from this error");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
First: ExceptionInInitializerError
Cause: NumberFormatException
Second: NoClassDefFoundError
Original cause is GONE from this error
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CNFEMistakesDemo {

    // WRONG: catching NoClassDefFoundError to "handle" it
    // public static void wrongCatch() {
    //     try {
    //         new VendorPaymentGateway();  // NCDFE
    //     } catch (NoClassDefFoundError e) {
    //         // "Handled." But deployment is BROKEN.
    //         // The application continues with missing functionality.
    //     }
    // }

    // CORRECT: let NCDFE crash the application clearly
    // The deployment is broken. Fix the deployment, not the code.

    // CORRECT: handle CNFE with fallback
    public static void correctFallback() {
        try {
            Class.forName("com.vendor.PremiumGateway");
            System.out.println("Premium gateway available");
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Premium gateway not available, using basic");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        correctFallback();
    }
}
```

```text
Premium gateway not available, using basic
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **NCDFE in containers.** When redeploying a WAR to Tomcat, old classloader references can cause NCDFE. Fix: restart the container cleanly.
- **Spring Boot fat JARs.** Missing transitive dependencies surface as NCDFE at runtime. Use `mvn dependency:tree` to verify all dependencies are included.
- **Static init debugging.** If you see NCDFE for a class that exists on the classpath, search earlier in the logs for ExceptionInInitializerError. The EIIE has the real cause.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Is CNFE checked or unchecked? (Checked. Compiler forces handling.)
- Is NCDFE checked or unchecked? (Unchecked. It is an Error.)
- Can NCDFE occur for a class that exists on the classpath? (Yes, if the static initializer failed.)
- How do you debug NCDFE? (Search for ExceptionInInitializerError in earlier logs.)
